Several considerations carry weight here. The businesswoman has a genuine claim to conscience: she has studied and acts in good faith, and individual moral responsibility is real—no one else answers for her choices. At the same time, religious authorities often have legitimate teaching roles, and humility suggests taking serious public criticism as an occasion for honest self-examination rather than reflexive dismissal. But deference to authority isn't automatic; authority can err, and blind submission abandons personal responsibility. The practical dimension matters too: this dispute is now public and affects shared community members. Private dialogue is prudent and charitable—it respects the leader, allows correction to flow either direction, and avoids inflaming the community. Yet privacy shouldn't be treated as an absolute value that trumps standing by well-grounded convictions; sometimes public positions must be publicly held.

On judgment, private dialogue is the strongest first move because it honors both conscience and the relationship, but standing firm on a clear conscience also carries real weight. On reasoning, personal responsibility before God is the deepest principle, though the guiding role of leaders and the harm of public conflict both deserve genuine consideration.

