Several considerations carry weight here. Engineers' unanimous technical judgment about safety is highly relevant—water treatment involves genuine expertise that laypeople reading articles typically can't match, and getting the safety facts wrong has real consequences. But legitimacy in democratic governance matters too: residents are the ones bearing the risk, and forcing something on an unwilling public tends to backfire, erode trust, and is itself a kind of harm. The crucial detail is that the residents' concerns are "unfounded"—not a values disagreement but a factual error. That points strongly toward closing the information gap rather than either overriding the public or deferring to a mistaken majority. Time invested in genuine education respects both the facts and the people. This isn't merely a compromise; it addresses the actual root of the problem.

That said, education isn't guaranteed to succeed, and pure deference to a factually mistaken majority on a safety matter carries less weight than deference on a values matter would.

