On reasoning: the function of consensus (bias protection) and the existence of legitimate challenge processes both carry real weight, and they point toward the same action. The "obligation to the patient over protocols" framing is partly right—the patient does come first—but it's often used to justify overriding safeguards, when protocols largely exist *to serve* patients. So it matters, but less than the structured considerations.

