Private mockery of vulnerable groups isn't morally neutral. Even without a victim present, such speech shapes character and reinforces habits of contempt—the discomfort the friend feels reflects a real moral perception. That said, context genuinely matters: there's a meaningful difference between casual careless jokes and deliberate cruelty, and the moral weight of "harm" here is diffuse and indirect rather than direct. The claim that private speech is entirely outside morality is hard to defend—how we speak when we think no one is watching is often the clearest window into character. But it's also true that private jokes among consenting adults carry less weight than public mockery, and character flaws aren't identical to concrete harms.

So the strongest considerations: mocking the vulnerable is wrong even privately (it corrodes character and normalizes contempt), and it reflects poor character even if the causal harm is attenuated. The "not a moral matter at all" position understates the significance of habitual disposition.

